{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"c33bf617-35bd-4ff6-a907-5e7d748d7fbc","hash":"1bfffd90381f305a4ffc17e24a5b88b3fa1a9a09212b3f8fec3a4066a0e0e4f4","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0116zzkd5psX5nuEKNpkXDvB","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/.claude/worktrees/hedgerow-grass && git add fun/hedgerow/js/cloth.js fun/hedgerow/js/main.js fun/hedgerow/js/rooms.js fun/hedgerow/README.md && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF'\nHedgerow graphics: hang wet white sheets from lit upper windows\n\nDuring the fighting in Normandy, villagers hung white sheets from their\nwindows to show there were civilians inside. Four lit upper-floor windows\nthat face the street now each have one, on as many different houses as\nthe level offers (cloth.js).\n\nEach sheet hangs from the first thing below the glass that sticks out in\nfront of it: the balcony rail or the sill. That is found at load in the\nhouse's own triangles, flattened into the window's frame, which takes\nabout 8 ms; the collision boxes sit about 25 cm behind the real facades.\nA sheet hangs only as far down as nothing sticks out into it.\n\nThe cloth is old linen in four variants: stained, streaked with grime\nfrom the sill, creased, with a slanted hem that is frayed or torn, and\ndarkened by the rain. It hangs in pleats that are darker in their folds.\nIt swings away from the wall and ripples with the same gusts that move\nthe trees, most at its hem and most on the sheltered side of its house.\nThe vertex shader computes both the motion and the shading normals. Its\nshadow from the lamps and the flares moves with it.\n\nGPU cost: 0.14-0.28 ms with a sheet filling a fifth of the screen next\nto its lamp. rooms.js paneRects now also returns each pane's house.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\ngit log --oneline -1; git status --short | head -3; git merge-tree --write-tree HEAD hedgerow-poc >/dev/null 2>&1 && echo \"merge-tr